🔗 1. What is an HTML Link?

An HTML link allows users to move from one webpage or location to another.

For example:

- Open Google
- Open GitHub
- Open another page
- Open a specific section
- Send an email
- Call a phone number

HTML links are created using the "<a>" element.

Example:

<a href="https://www.google.com">
    Google
</a>

---

🏷️ 2. Anchor Element "<a>"

The "<a>" element is called the Anchor element.

It is mainly used to create hyperlinks.

Basic structure:

<a href="URL">
    Link Text
</a>

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    GitHub
</a>

Here:

<a> → Opening tag
href → Attribute
"https://github.com" → Attribute value
GitHub → Link text
</a> → Closing tag

Remember:

<a> = Anchor = Link

---

⚙️ 3. href Attribute

"href" stands for Hypertext Reference.

It tells the browser where the link should go.

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    Visit GitHub
</a>

Here:

href="https://github.com"

is the destination of the link.

Basic syntax:

<a href="URL">
    Link Text
</a>

---

📝 4. Link Text

The text between the opening and closing "<a>" tags is called the link text.

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    Visit My GitHub
</a>

Here:

Visit My GitHub

is the visible link text.

It is better to use meaningful link text.

Better:

<a href="https://github.com">
    Visit My GitHub Profile
</a>

Instead of:

<a href="https://github.com">
    Click Here
</a>

Meaningful link text helps users understand where the link will take them.

---

🌐 5. External Links

An external link points to another website or domain.

Example:

<a href="https://www.google.com">
    Google
</a>

Another example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    GitHub
</a>

Another:

<a href="https://www.freecodecamp.org">
    freeCodeCamp
</a>

---

🏠 6. Internal Links

An internal link connects pages within the same website/project.

Suppose our project has:

website/
│
├── index.html
├── about.html
└── contact.html

From "index.html" to "about.html":

<a href="about.html">
    About Me
</a>

From "index.html" to "contact.html":

<a href="contact.html">
    Contact
</a>

These are internal links because they point to pages inside the same website/project.

---

📁 7. What is a Relative Path?

A relative path describes the location of a file relative to the current file.

Example:

<a href="about.html">
    About
</a>

If "about.html" is in the same folder, we can simply write:

about.html

Another example:

<a href="pages/contact.html">
    Contact
</a>

Here:

pages/contact.html

is a relative path.

---

🌍 8. What is an Absolute URL?

An absolute URL contains the complete web address.

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    GitHub
</a>

Here:

https://github.com

is an absolute URL.

Difference:

Relative path:

about.html

Absolute URL:

https://example.com/about.html

Remember:

Relative → Based on current location

Absolute → Complete address

---

↩️ 9. Parent Directory "../"

"../" is used to move one folder level up in a relative path.

Example structure:

website/
│
├── index.html
│
└── pages/
    └── about.html

From "about.html" to "index.html":

<a href="../index.html">
    Home
</a>

Here:

../

means:

Go one level up.

Simple trick:

../ = Parent folder

---

🎯 10. target Attribute

The "target" attribute controls the browsing context in which a link is opened.

Example:

<a
    href="https://github.com"
    target="_blank"
>
    GitHub
</a>

---

🆕 11. target="_blank"

"target="_blank" is commonly used to open a link in a new browser tab or another new browsing context.

Example:

<a
    href="https://github.com"
    target="_blank"
>
    Visit GitHub
</a>

Important:

"target="_blank" does not mean that every browser must visually create a new tab. It requests a new browsing context, which commonly appears as a new tab.

---

🔒 12. rel Attribute

The "rel" attribute describes the relationship between the current page and the linked resource.

For external links opened with "_blank", a common pattern is:

<a
    href="https://github.com"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
>
    GitHub
</a>

"noopener" and "noreferrer" can provide security and privacy-related behavior for new browsing contexts.

---

📧 13. Email Links

We can create an email link using:

mailto:

Example:

<a href="mailto:example@gmail.com">
    Send Email
</a>

When the user clicks the link, the browser/device may open an email application or email handler.

Example:

<a href="mailto:shravan@example.com">
    Email Shravan
</a>

---

📞 14. Telephone Links

We can create a phone link using:

tel:

Example:

<a href="tel:+911234567890">
    Call Me
</a>

On supported devices, clicking the link can open the phone/calling interface.

---

🔖 15. Link to a Section of the Same Page

We can create a link that jumps to a specific section of the same webpage.

For this we use:

id

and

#

Example:

<a href="#about">
    Go to About
</a>

<h2 id="about">
    About Me
</h2>

Here:

href="#about"

points to:

id="about"

---

🧭 16. Creating Page Navigation

Example:

<nav>

    <a href="#home">
        Home
    </a>

    <a href="#about">
        About
    </a>

    <a href="#skills">
        Skills
    </a>

    <a href="#contact">
