🖼️ 17. What is an HTML Image?

Images can be displayed on a webpage using the "<img>" element.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

The browser loads the image specified in the "src" attribute.

---

🏷️ 18. "<img>" Element

Basic syntax:

<img
    src="image.jpg"
    alt="Image description"
>

The "<img>" element is a void element.

It does not normally have a closing tag.

Correct:

<img src="photo.jpg" alt="My Photo">

Incorrect:

<img src="photo.jpg" alt="My Photo"></img>

---

📍 19. src Attribute

"src" means Source.

It tells the browser where the image/resource is located.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Profile photo"
>

Here:

src="profile.jpg"

is the image source.

---

♿ 20. alt Attribute

"alt" means alternative text.

It provides a text description of an image.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

Why is "alt" important?

- Helps when an image cannot be displayed
- Helps screen-reader users understand meaningful images
- Improves accessibility
- Describes the purpose/meaning of the image

Good:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

Weak:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="image"
>

For a purely decorative image, an empty alt can be appropriate:

<img
    src="decoration.png"
    alt=""
>

---

📏 21. Image Width

We can specify an image width using the "width" attribute.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar"
    width="200"
>

Here:

width="200"

sets the image width to 200 CSS pixels in this HTML presentation.

For responsive designs, CSS is generally preferred for controlling image size.

---

📐 22. Image Height

We can specify image height using the "height" attribute.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar"
    width="200"
    height="200"
>

Here:

width="200"

and:

height="200"

specify the displayed dimensions.

---

💻 23. Local Images

A local image is an image stored inside our project.

Example:

day02/
│
├── index.html
└── profile.jpg

HTML:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

Because the image is in the same folder as "index.html", we can use:

profile.jpg

---

📁 24. Images Folder

For better project organization, images can be stored in a separate folder.

Example:

day02/
│
├── index.html
│
└── images/
    └── profile.jpg

HTML:

<img
    src="images/profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

Here:

images/profile.jpg

is the relative path.

---

📂 25. Multiple Folder Path

Suppose the structure is:

project/
│
├── index.html
│
└── assets/
    │
    └── images/
        └── profile.jpg

Then:

<img
    src="assets/images/profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

Path:

assets/images/profile.jpg

---

🌐 26. Online Images

An image can also be loaded using a complete URL.

Example:

<img
    src="https://example.com/image.jpg"
    alt="Example image"
>

Here:

https://example.com/image.jpg

is an absolute URL.

For personal projects, storing important images inside your own project is often more reliable because you control the files.

---

🔗 27. Image as a Link

An image can be placed inside an anchor element.

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">

    <img
        src="github-logo.png"
        alt="GitHub"
        width="100"
    >

</a>

Now the image becomes clickable.

Clicking the image opens GitHub.

---

⚙️ 28. What are HTML Attributes?

Attributes provide additional information about HTML elements.

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">
    GitHub
</a>

Here:

href

is an attribute.

Another example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Profile photo"
>

Here:

src

and:

alt

are attributes.

---

🧩 29. Attribute Syntax

General syntax:

<tag attribute="value">

Example:

<a href="https://github.com">

Another example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Profile photo"
>

Basic structure:

attribute="value"

Example:

href="https://github.com"

href → Attribute name

"https://github.com" → Attribute value

---

➕ 30. Multiple Attributes

One HTML element can have multiple attributes.

Example:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
    width="200"
    height="200"
>

Here we have:

src
alt
width
height

Four attributes.

---

🆔 31. id Attribute

The "id" attribute gives an element a unique identifier within a page.

Example:

<h2 id="about">
    About Me
</h2>

The id can be used for:

- Page navigation
- CSS
- JavaScript

Example:

<a href="#about">
    Go to About
</a>

<h2 id="about">
    About Me
</h2>

---

🏷️ 32. class Attribute

The "class" attribute is used to assign one or more elements to a class.

Example:

<p class="skill">
    HTML
</p>

<p class="skill">
    CSS
</p>

<p class="skill">
    JavaScript
</p>

The same class can be used on multiple elements.

Later in CSS:

.skill {
    color: blue;
}

---

📝 33. title Attribute

The "title" attribute can provide additional information about an element.

Example:

<a
    href="https://github.com"
    title="Open GitHub"
>
    GitHub
</a>

In many browsers, hovering over the element may show the title as a tooltip.

---

🎨 34. style Attribute

The "style" attribute allows inline CSS.

Example:

<p style="color: blue;">
    Hello Shravan
</p>

The text will be styled using the inline CSS rule.

Important:

Inline style is useful for learning and small cases, but larger projects should generally use CSS files.

Example:

style="color: blue;"

Later we will learn:

CSS

and use:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

---

📥 35. download Attribute

The "download" attribute can request download behavior for a linked resource.

Example:

<a
    href="resume.pdf"
    download
>
    Download Resume
</a>

It can also have a value in some cases:

<a
    href="resume.pdf"
    download="Shravan-Kumar-Resume.pdf"
>
    Download Resume
</a>

Important:

Actual download behavior can depend on browser and server/security rules, especially for cross-origin resources.

---

↩️ 36. "<br>" Tag

"<br>" creates a line break.

Example:

Hello<br>
My name is Shravan.

Output:

Hello
My name is Shravan.

Another example:

<p>
    Hello Shravan<br>
    Welcome to my website.
</p>

Important:

Do not use many "<br>" tags to create page layout or large spacing.

CSS should be used for layout and spacing.

---

➖ 37. "<hr>" Tag

"<hr>" represents a thematic break between sections.

Example:

<h2>About Me</h2>

<hr>

<h2>My Skills</h2>

It can help visually and semantically separate topics or sections.

---

🚫 38. Void Elements

Void elements are HTML elements that do not have normal closing tags.

Common examples:

<img>
<br>
<hr>
<input>
<meta>
<link>
<source>
<track>
<wbr>

Examples:

<img src="photo.jpg" alt="Photo">

<br>

<hr>

<input type="text">

We do not normally write:

</img>

</br>

</hr>

---

🔄 39. Relative Path vs Absolute URL

Relative path:

about.html

images/profile.jpg

../index.html

Absolute URL:

https://github.com

https://www.google.com

Easy way to remember:

Relative
→ Current project/file location ke according

Absolute
→ Complete address

