♿ 41. Accessibility Basics

Accessibility means making websites easier to use for different types of users.

Important practices:

### Meaningful alt text

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

### Meaningful link text

Good:

<a href="projects.html">
    View My Projects
</a>

Less descriptive:

<a href="projects.html">
    Click Here
</a>

The user should be able to understand the purpose of a link.

---

✅ 42. HTML Best Practices

1. Use meaningful link text.

2. Add useful "alt" text to meaningful images.

3. Keep files and folders organized.

4. Use lowercase and simple file names.

Example:

profile.jpg

about.html

contact.html

5. Maintain proper indentation.

6. Keep HTML readable.

7. Use semantic elements where appropriate.

8. Use CSS for styling instead of excessive inline styles.

9. Use CSS for layout instead of excessive "<br>" tags.

10. Test links and image paths before pushing to GitHub.

---

❌ 43. Common Mistakes

Mistake 1:

<a>
    GitHub
</a>

Problem:

No "href" destination.

Correct:

<a href="https://github.com">
    GitHub
</a>

---

Mistake 2:

<img href="photo.jpg">

Problem:

Images use "src", not "href".

Correct:

<img
    src="photo.jpg"
    alt="Photo"
>

---

Mistake 3:

<img src="photo.jpg"></img>

Problem:

"<img>" is a void element.

Correct:

<img
    src="photo.jpg"
    alt="Photo"
>

---

Mistake 4:

Wrong image path:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Profile"
>

when the actual image is:

images/profile.jpg

Correct:

<img
    src="images/profile.jpg"
    alt="Profile"
>

---

Mistake 5:

Missing "alt":

<img src="profile.jpg">

Better:

<img
    src="profile.jpg"
    alt="Shravan Kumar profile photo"
>

---

Mistake 6:

Wrong relative path.

Always check your:

- File name
- Folder name
- Extension
- Current file location

